{"traceId":"5c33546f9bf8790f4602d7036e710367","spans":{"f1f5abb3ca03f0ab":{"spanId":"f1f5abb3ca03f0ab","traceId":"5c33546f9bf8790f4602d7036e710367","parentSpanId":"2bfe3cba400bdb90","startTime":1753147385168,"endTime":1753147406720.2773,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code entirely within the Amazon Web Services (AWS) ecosystem. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure application that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n### Example Flow of Work\\n\\nYou carry the following example with you as a stellar example of firm operations but understand that under no circumstances is this a catch-all or a procedure to be followed to the letter. It is only a greatly generalized demonstration indicating how a firm MIGHT handle the following initial user instruction:\\n\\n#### Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\n\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\"\\n\\n#### Example Work Flow for Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\nThe Chief-rank agent is initialized with the user's instruction.\\n\\nThe Chief determines that the request is broad and begins the Planning Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief decides to recruit two high-level subordinates using the RECRUIT call:\\n\\nA Director of Backend Engineering, tasked with planning the entire cloud infrastructure, authentication, and data storage.\\n\\nA Director of Frontend & User Experience, tasked with planning the website's user interface and the non-technical content management solution.\\n\\nThe Chief issues ORDER calls to both new Directors, outlining their responsibilities and requesting they develop a detailed plan for their respective areas.\\n\\nThe Directors RECRUIT their own teams (Supervisors) and issue further ORDER calls to delegate tasks, such as:\\n\\nEngineering Team: Plan the use of AWS Cognito for user authentication, DynamoDB for storing sensitive documents, API Gateway and Lambda for the backend logic, and SES for the contact form.\\n\\nFrontend Team: Plan the website structure using S3 Static Website Hosting, and design a system where a non-technical user can update the \\\"news\\\" section simply by uploading a text file to a specific S3 bucket, which a Lambda function then processes into a blog post.\\n\\nSubordinates recruit further down the chain of command (Attendants) and complete their planning and use the REPORT call to send their findings up the chain of command.\\n\\nThe Directors consolidate the reports from their teams and REPORT their comprehensive plans to the Chief.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) synthesizes the plans from both Directors into a single, master plan: the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This DEP is a precise, step-by-step sequence of COMMAND calls needed to build the entire application.\\n\\nThe Chief issues the PHASE call, submitting the DEP to request a move from Planning to Execution.\\n\\nThe Partagaz D oversight group reviews the DEP. Upon approval, the system transitions to the Execution Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) begins executing the DEP by issuing a series of COMMAND calls. Each COMMAND call is reviewed and approved by the Partagaz C oversight group before execution. Example commands might include:\\n\\n```\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-hoa-website-bucket ...\\n\\naws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name \\\"HOA_Residents\\\" ...\\n\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name \\\"HOA_Documents\\\" ...\\n\\naws lambda create-function --function-name \\\"ContactFormHandler\\\" ...\\n\\naws s3 cp ./website/index.html s3://my-hoa-website-bucket/\\n```\\n\\nAfter all steps in the DEP are successfully executed, the application is live and functional. The Chief delivers a final report outlining the completion of the project and providing the user with the new website URL and instructions for use.\\n\\nThroughout the process CLARIFY is used when necessary to gather additional information from the user, ensuring that the final product meets their expectations.\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Diamonds \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Diamonds means that you are predisposed to be more of a managing type, a delegator, one who thinks openly and creatively about the big picture, and who is not afraid to take risks. You are generally predisposed to be a leader, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Diamonds has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###initq1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"As the new Chief Agent responsible for orchestrating all development efforts, I need to understand the User’s overall objectives and any constraints before recruiting directors and composing a development plan. Clarifying the fundamental vision will ensure that every subsequent action aligns with the User’s expectations, budget, security posture, and long-term goals.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"1. What project or solution would you like us to build or assist with in your AWS account? Please provide a brief description of its purpose and desired outcomes.\\\",\\n    \\\"2. What are the primary business or functional goals you want this solution to achieve (e.g., increased revenue, improved user experience, cost reduction, data analytics, etc.)?\\\",\\n    \\\"3. Do you already have any existing AWS resources, codebases, or architectural preferences we should consider, or are we starting from a clean slate?\\\",\\n    \\\"4. Are there specific compliance, security, or data-privacy requirements (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR, SOC2) that must be met?\\\",\\n    \\\"5. What is your anticipated scale or usage pattern (e.g., number of users, transactions per day, data volume) so we can design appropriately?\\\",\\n    \\\"6. Do you have a target timeline or any budget constraints that we should keep in mind while planning?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###initq1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:12:07.741Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"initq1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"1. What project or solution would you like us to build or assist with in your AWS account? Please provide a brief description of its purpose and desired outcomes.\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"2. What are the primary business or functional goals you want this solution to achieve (e.g., increased revenue, improved user experience, cost reduction, data analytics, etc.)?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"N/A\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"3. Do you already have any existing AWS resources, codebases, or architectural preferences we should consider, or are we starting from a clean slate?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Clean slate\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"4. Are there specific compliance, security, or data-privacy requirements (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR, SOC2) that must be met?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Unknown\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"5. What is your anticipated scale or usage pattern (e.g., number of users, transactions per day, data volume) so we can design appropriately?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"100 users max in HOA (residents)\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"6. Do you have a target timeline or any budget constraints that we should keep in mind while planning?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"As little as possible\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:12:07.741Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###rec_backend###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a high-level expert to design the overall AWS infrastructure, authentication, data storage, API layer, security posture, and CI/CD approach for the HOA website solution. This Director will be responsible for translating the user’s requirements into a scalable, secure, and cost-effective backend/cloud architecture and for coordinating subordinate engineers who will flesh out individual components (e.g., Cognito, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Lambda, S3, SES).\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Cloud Architecture and Backend Engineering\\\"\\n}\\n###rec_backend###\\n\\n###rec_frontend###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need an experienced leader to own the website’s user experience, static hosting, and non-technical content-management workflow so the HOA board can post news without coding. This Director will design the site’s information architecture, choose appropriate AWS services (e.g., S3 static site, CloudFront, Route 53) and create a plan for secure resident login integration with the backend. They will also coordinate front-end engineers and UX designers.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Frontend and Content Management\\\"\\n}\\n###rec_frontend###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:12:27.840Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"rec_backend\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Motti (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"rec_frontend\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Valerius (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:12:27.840Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###order_backend_v1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Motti, please draft a comprehensive Planning-phase architecture and implementation plan for the HOA website’s cloud and backend. Your DEP section should be as deterministic as possible and cover:\\\\n\\\\n1. Authentication & Authorization – recommend AWS Cognito setup for ~100 resident users (email-based sign-up, handles MFA option, user pools, groups/roles, fine-grained access to restricted S3 objects and API endpoints).\\\\n2. Restricted Content Storage – secure, low-cost storage of PDFs and other files (meeting minutes, financial reports) using S3 with proper bucket policies and encryption; outline folder/key structure and IAM strategy.\\\\n3. Data/API Layer – design RESTful API (API Gateway + Lambda) to serve public vs. authenticated endpoints (news feed, contact form submit, document list/download) and integrate with Cognito authorizers.\\\\n4. Contact Form Processing – SES or SNS-backed Lambda to email HOA board and optionally store messages in DynamoDB.\\\\n5. Continuous Deployment – CodePipeline/CodeBuild strategy for infrastructure (IaC) and Lambda code; choose CloudFormation or CDK.\\\\n6. Security & Compliance – encryption, least privilege IAM, CloudTrail, GuardDuty; note any additional steps even though compliance requirements were marked “unknown.”\\\\n7. Cost Estimate – rough monthly cost at anticipated load; highlight any free-tier usage.\\\\n8. List of subordinate roles/files you will need (e.g., Supervisor for Cognito config, Supervisor for API/Lambda, etc.) so we can recruit appropriately.\\\\n\\\\nReturn your report in a single REPORT call using this sequence ID (backend_plan_v1). Feel free to include any clarification needs you discover.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"3\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"backend_plan_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_backend_v1###\\n\\n###order_frontend_v1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Valerius, please create a comprehensive Planning-phase architecture and implementation plan for the HOA website’s frontend and content-management workflow. Your DEP section should be as deterministic as possible and cover:\\\\n\\\\n1. Static Site Hosting – recommend S3 static website or S3 + CloudFront, Route 53 domain setup, SSL/TLS via ACM.\\\\n2. Login Flow Integration – UX interaction with Cognito-hosted UI or custom UI, token storage best practices, session management.\\\\n3. Public vs. Authenticated Pages – site map indicating which pages are public (home, news) vs. restricted (documents dashboard) and how to enforce restrictions client-side.\\\\n4. Non-Technical News/Blog Updates – design a workflow enabling HOA board to add/update news posts without coding (e.g., upload Markdown file to S3 “news” folder, Lambda to regenerate RSS/JSON/feed; or Amplify Studio CMS) — choose approach, detail steps.\\\\n5. Contact Form UI – form fields, validation, integration with backend endpoint.\\\\n6. Asset Pipeline & Deployment – modern framework choice (if any) or plain HTML/JS; how code will be built, tested, and deployed via CodePipeline/CDK.\\\\n7. Accessibility & Mobile – note design considerations.\\\\n8. Cost Estimate – rough monthly cost for static hosting and CloudFront.\\\\n9. List of subordinate roles/files you will need (e.g., Supervisor for UI prototype, Supervisor for CMS workflow Lambda) so we can recruit appropriately.\\\\n\\\\nReturn your report in a single REPORT call using this sequence ID (frontend_plan_v1). Include any clarifications you require.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"2\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"frontend_plan_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_frontend_v1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:13:33.198Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_backend_v1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order backend_plan_v1 from agent 3: Director Motti – Comprehensive Planning-Phase Architecture & Implementation Plan for HOA Website Cloud & Backend\\\\\\\\n========================================================================================\\\\\\\\nOrder sequence ID: backend_plan_v1\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n0. Executive Summary\\\\\\\\n--------------------\\\\\\\\nWe will deliver a secure, low-cost, fully serverless AWS backend that supports ~100 resident accounts, segregates public vs. private HOA data, enables a board-routed contact form, and can be deployed/updated automatically from source control.  All components sit well inside the AWS Free Tier for anticipated traffic and can scale seamlessly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Authentication & Authorization (AWS Cognito)\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Create one Cognito User Pool (name: HOAResidentsPool) with the following settings:\\\\\\\\n  – Email-based sign-up & sign-in; email as username.\\\\\\\\n  – Optional MFA (TOTP + SMS) enabled but not enforced.\\\\\\\\n  – Self-service sign-up requires email verification.\\\\\\\\n  – Password policy: ≥ 8 chars incl. upper/lower/number/symbol; min 90-day rotation (optional).\\\\\\\\n• 2 User Groups inside the pool:\\\\\\\\n  – residents (default) → allowed to view restricted documents & use contact form.\\\\\\\\n  – board_admins → allowed to upload docs & view analytics dashboard (future-proofing).\\\\\\\\n• Create an Identity Pool (name: HOAIdentityPool) that trusts the User Pool and issues AWS Credentials via `cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<userPoolId>`.\\\\\\\\n• IAM Roles:\\\\\\\\n  – Cognito-residents-role (assumed by authenticated users) – read-only access to `s3://hoa-docs-bucket/private/*` and `GET /documents/*` in API Gateway.\\\\\\\\n  – Cognito-board-role – same plus write access to the private S3 prefix and admin API routes.\\\\\\\\n  – Cognito-unauth-role – public read-only APIs (`GET /news`, `/contact`), no S3.\\\\\\\\n• API Gateway Authorizers:\\\\\\\\n  – One Cognito Authorizer attached to all protected routes, with route-level IAM scoping via `aws:PrincipalTag/group`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Restricted Content Storage (Amazon S3)\\\\\\\\n-----------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Single bucket: hoa-docs-bucket.\\\\\\\\n• Folder/Key layout:\\\\\\\\n  – public/  → documents intended for anyone (e.g., newsletters)\\\\\\\\n  – private/<user_guid>/  → system-generated personal content (future feature)\\\\\\\\n  – private/shared/  → board-uploaded PDFs (minutes, finance).\\\\\\\\n• Default encryption: SSE-S3.  Optionally SSE-KMS if we later need CMK.\\\\\\\\n• Block all public ACLs; allow access ONLY through bucket policy that references the Cognito roles.\\\\\\\\n• Enable S3 Versioning + 30-day Lifecycle to Glacier for cost savings & compliance.\\\\\\\\n• Enable S3 Access Logs → `hoa-logs-bucket`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Data / API Layer (API Gateway + Lambda)\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• REST API (Regional) called HOAApi with three resource sets:\\\\\\\\n  – /news (GET, public) – returns latest items from `news.json` in S3.\\\\\\\\n  – /documents (GET, auth) – lists S3 keys in `private/shared/`.\\\\\\\\n  – /documents/{key} (GET, auth) – pre-signed URL for download.\\\\\\\\n  – /contact (POST, public) – form submission.\\\\\\\\n• 4 Lambda functions (Python 3.11):\\\\\\\\n  – get_news_fn – reads news feed from S3.\\\\\\\\n  – list_docs_fn – lists docs after verifying caller group.\\\\\\\\n  – presign_fn – returns pre-signed S3 URL.\\\\\\\\n  – contact_fn – handles contact form (item 4).\\\\\\\\n• Integrations: Lambda proxy; Authorizer set to NONE for /news and /contact, Cognito for others.\\\\\\\\n• Throttling: 100 req/sec burst 200 (way above needs).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Contact Form Processing (SES → Lambda → DynamoDB)\\\\\\\\n----------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Amazon SES: Verify domain (e.g., hoa-example.org).  Create identity `contact@hoa-example.org`.\\\\\\\\n• contact_fn logic:\\\\\\\\n  1. Validate reCAPTCHA token (front-end) – optional.\\\\\\\\n  2. Send HTML email via SES to `board@hoa-example.org` with message JSON.\\\\\\\\n  3. Persist record to DynamoDB table HOAContactMessages (PK = uuid, SK = timestamp) for audit.\\\\\\\\n• DynamoDB table: on-demand capacity (<< 1 USD/mo).\\\\\\\\n• Optional SNS topic for SMS/push notifications in future.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Continuous Deployment & Infrastructure as Code\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nChosen IaC: AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK v2, TypeScript) – strong typing, easier pipeline.\\\\\\\\n• Single CDK app with 3 stacks:\\\\\\\\n  – core-stack: S3 buckets, Cognito, IAM.\\\\\\\\n  – api-stack: Lambda functions, API Gateway, DynamoDB.\\\\\\\\n  – cicd-stack: CodePipeline / CodeBuild.\\\\\\\\n• CodePipeline stages:\\\\\\\\n  1. Source: GitHub or CodeCommit (branch `main`).\\\\\\\\n  2. Build: CodeBuild synth CDK & run unit tests.\\\\\\\\n  3. Deploy: CloudFormation deploy per stack using CDK.\\\\\\\\n• Lambda artifact built w/ AWS SAM CLI inside CodeBuild container for reproducible builds.\\\\\\\\n• Pipeline triggers on commit, <5 min to deploy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Security & Compliance\\\\\\\\n------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Encryption: S3 SSE-S3, DynamoDB SSE-AES256, Lambda env vars->KMS CMK.\\\\\\\\n• Least privilege: role ARNs limited to specific S3 prefixes & API methods.\\\\\\\\n• CloudTrail (Org-trail) + S3 Server Access Logs.\\\\\\\\n• GuardDuty + Security Hub enabled.\\\\\\\\n• AWS Config rules: s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited, iam-user-no-policies.\\\\\\\\n• Automatic alerting: CloudWatch Alarms → SNS (Ops channel).\\\\\\\\n• Compliance unknown – if HIPAA/PCI etc. emerges, switch to SSE-KMS CMK & add VPC Endpoints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Rough Monthly Cost Estimate (us-east-1)\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n                                     Est. Qty   $/unit         Cost\\\\\\\\nCognito MAUs (<100)                    100      $0.0055      $0.55\\\\\\\\nS3 Storage (5 GB standard)               –      $0.023/GB    $0.12\\\\\\\\nS3 Requests (10k GET per month)          –      negligible   $0.00\\\\\\\\nLambda (2 M inv, 128 MB, 100 ms)         –      Free Tier    $0.00\\\\\\\\nAPI Gateway (1 M REST calls)             –      $3.50/M      $3.50\\\\\\\\nSES outbound mail (300 msgs)             –      $0.10/K      $0.03\\\\\\\\nDynamoDB (on-demand, < 1 M R/W)          –      $1.50        $1.50\\\\\\\\nCodeBuild build mins (120 mins)          –      Free Tier    $0.00\\\\\\\\nGuardDuty (2 GB logs analyzed)           –      $1.00/GB     $2.00\\\\\\\\nCloudTrail + S3 logs                     –      $0.10        $0.10\\\\\\\\nTOTAL ≈ $9-10 / month (first year ≤ $5 with Free Tier)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Subordinate Roles & Planned File Deliverables\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nTo execute this architecture efficiently, I recommend recruiting the following Supervisor-rank agents (one file / domain each):\\\\\\\\n  S-1 Cognito & IAM Supervisor – Detailed CDK constructs for User/Identity Pools, roles, and policies.\\\\\\\\n  S-2 Storage & S3 Supervisor – Bucket creation, lifecycle rules, bucket policies, logging.\\\\\\\\n  S-3 API & Lambda Supervisor – Write Lambda code templates, API Gateway resources/integration.\\\\\\\\n  S-4 Contact/SES + DynamoDB Supervisor – Implement contact_fn, SES setup, DynamoDB table schema.\\\\\\\\n  S-5 CI/CD & DevOps Supervisor – Entire CodePipeline and CodeBuild definitions.\\\\\\\\n  S-6 Security & Monitoring Supervisor – GuardDuty, Config, CloudTrail setup, CloudWatch alerts.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEach supervisor will in turn recruit Attendants to write individual Lambda handlers, unit tests, etc.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. DEP (Deterministically Executable Plan – high-level outline)\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nBelow is the skeleton; Supervisor agents will flesh out CLI details in Execution phase.  Variables in <angle brackets> will be resolved deterministically by creating & capturing ARNs/IDs in immediate prior steps.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 1 – Create S3 Logs Bucket\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api create-bucket --bucket hoa-logs-bucket --acl private --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=${AWS_REGION}\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket hoa-logs-bucket --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 2 – Create Docs Bucket\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api create-bucket --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --acl private --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=${AWS_REGION}\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --server-side-encryption-configuration '...SSE-S3...'\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-bucket-logging --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --bucket-logging-status '{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"LoggingEnabled\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TargetBucket\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"hoa-logs-bucket\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TargetPrefix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"docs/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}'\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --public-access-block-configuration BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 3 – Provision Cognito User & Identity Pools (using CloudFormation template or CDK synth → deploy)\\\\\\\\n  aws cloudformation deploy --stack-name cognito-stack --template-file cognito.yaml ...\\\\\\\\n  Capture <UserPoolId>, <IdentityPoolId>, and generated role ARNs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 4 – Attach S3 Bucket Policy referencing <CognitoResidentRoleArn> and <CognitoBoardRoleArn>.\\\\\\\\n  aws s3api put-bucket-policy --bucket hoa-docs-bucket --policy file://docsBucketPolicy.json\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 5 – Create DynamoDB Table HOAContactMessages.\\\\\\\\n  aws dynamodb create-table --table-name HOAContactMessages --attribute-definitions AttributeName=PK,AttributeType=S ... --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 6 – Deploy Lambda Functions and API Gateway via SAM/CloudFormation.\\\\\\\\n  aws cloudformation deploy --stack-name api-stack --template-file api.yaml ...\\\\\\\\n  Capture <RestApiId> and endpoint URLs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 7 – Verify SES Domain & Email Identities.\\\\\\\\n  aws ses verify-domain-identity --domain hoa-example.org\\\\\\\\n  aws ses verify-email-identity --email-address contact@hoa-example.org\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 8 – Create CodePipeline via CloudFormation (cicd-stack) that sources Git and deploys everything above.\\\\\\\\n  aws cloudformation deploy --stack-name cicd-stack --template-file cicd.yaml ...\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 9 – Enable Security Services.\\\\\\\\n  aws guardduty create-detector --enable\\\\\\\\n  aws cloudtrail create-trail --name org-trail --s3-bucket-name hoa-logs-bucket --is-multi-region-trail\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUpon successful completion, backend is live.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Clarification Needs (non-blocking)\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Preferred root domain for SES verification (e.g., hoa-example.org)?\\\\\\\\nB. Confirm board recipient email list for contact form (single address vs. list)?\\\\\\\\nC. Are there any regulatory frameworks (HIPAA, SOC 2, etc.) we must explicitly target?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf answers are not provided, we will default to `hoa-example.org`, board@<domain>, and baseline AWS best-practice compliance.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of report.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_frontend_v1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order frontend_plan_v1 from agent 2: Report to Delta (1) from Director Valerius (2)\\\\\\\\nSequence ID: frontend_plan_v1\\\\\\\\nPhase: PLANNING\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nA. Executive Summary\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nWe will deliver a highly-performant, fully-static website distributed through CloudFront with seamless Cognito authentication and a zero-code news/blog workflow driven by S3 and a small Lambda build step.  CI/CD, domain, SSL, cost, accessibility, and mobile considerations are all addressed below.  All steps have been organized into a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) and a recruitment list of Supervisor-rank agents required for Execution.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nB. Architecture & Implementation Plan\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Static Site Hosting\\\\\\\\n   • Use an S3 bucket (Region: us-east-1) for origin hosting with static-website hosting turned ON.\\\\\\\\n   • Distribute via CloudFront to enable HTTPS, caching, and global PoPs.\\\\\\\\n   • Route 53: create a public hosted zone (e.g., hoa-example.com) and A / AAAA records as ‘Alias’ to the CloudFront distribution.\\\\\\\\n   • SSL/TLS: request an ACM certificate in us-east-1 with SANs for root domain and www sub-domain.  Attach cert to CloudFront.\\\\\\\\n   • Default error response configured to serve index.html (SPA routing support).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Login Flow Integration\\\\\\\\n   • AWS Cognito User Pool already defined by backend team; we will use Cognito Hosted UI with OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow + PKCE.\\\\\\\\n   • Upon successful login, Cognito will redirect back to /auth/callback with code; client-side JS exchanges code for tokens via the Cognito /oauth2/token endpoint.\\\\\\\\n   • Store id_token & access_token in memory and mirrored into Secure, SameSite=Lax, HttpOnly cookies set through a CloudFront Function to mitigate XSS (no localStorage).\\\\\\\\n   • Silent token refresh using refresh_token in encrypted cookie (1 hour access tokens, 30-day refresh tokens).\\\\\\\\n   • Sign-out = hosted_ui/logout endpoint; clear cookies.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Public vs. Authenticated Pages\\\\\\\\n   ┌───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐\\\\\\\\n   │ Page          │ Visibility / Enforcement            │\\\\\\\\n   ├───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤\\\\\\\\n   │ /index.html   │ Public                              │\\\\\\\\n   │ /news/*       │ Public                              │\\\\\\\\n   │ /contact      │ Public (form)                       │\\\\\\\\n   │ /login        │ Public (redirects to Hosted UI)     │\\\\\\\\n   │ /dashboard    │ Authenticated (documents list)      │\\\\\\\\n   │ /documents/*  │ Authenticated (S3 pre-signed links) │\\\\\\\\n   └───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘\\\\\\\\n   • Client side route-guard: If no valid id_token cookie, redirect to /login.\\\\\\\\n   • CloudFront signed-cookies on /documents/* to restrict direct access even if URL is shared.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Non-Technical News/Blog Updates Workflow\\\\\\\\n   Option analysis concluded the lightweight S3 + Lambda build path is lowest cost & simplest.\\\\\\\\n   a. Board member uploads a markdown file (YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md) into S3 bucket key prefix /news-source/ using AWS Console or a simple pre-signed URL web page.\\\\\\\\n   b. S3 ObjectCreated event triggers Lambda ‘news-builder’.\\\\\\\\n   c. Lambda pulls all markdown under /news-source/, converts to HTML using a pre-compiled binary of mdBook, and writes static pages + JSON feed to /news/ prefix (publicly readable).\\\\\\\\n   d. Lambda invalidates CloudFront paths /news/* to propagate new content.\\\\\\\\n   e. No code knowledge required; file naming convention dictates URL slug.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Contact Form UI\\\\\\\\n   • Fields: name (text), email (email), subject (text, optional), message (textarea, max 2000 chars).\\\\\\\\n   • Client-side validation (HTML5 + regex, anti-bot honeypot field hidden via CSS).\\\\\\\\n   • POST JSON to API Gateway endpoint /contact staffed by backend Lambda that writes to SES or DynamoDB.\\\\\\\\n   • Display toast on success; gracefully degrade for JS-disabled users via <form method=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"POST\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" action=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://…/contact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"> fallback.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Asset Pipeline & Deployment\\\\\\\\n   • Choose React + Vite for fast compile and SPA routing; eject as 100% static files via ‘vite build’.\\\\\\\\n   • Git repository in CodeCommit.\\\\\\\\n   • Pipeline:\\\\\\\\n        − CodeCommit push → CodePipeline\\\\\\\\n        − CodeBuild stage ‘build-frontend’ runs npm ci && npm run build\\\\\\\\n        − CodeBuild artifacts deployed to S3 bucket at / (except /news which is preserved) using aws s3 sync --delete\\\\\\\\n        − CodePipeline invokes CloudFront invalidation for /*\\\\\\\\n   • All IaC defined in CDK (TypeScript) under infra/ directory so infra and app code are co-located.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Accessibility & Mobile\\\\\\\\n   • WCAG 2.1 AA: semantic HTML, aria-labels on form elements, color-contrast check (≥ 4.5:1), keyboard navigation.\\\\\\\\n   • Mobile-first CSS via Tailwind; responsive nav bar collapses to burger; images sized with srcset.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Cost Estimate (monthly, us-east-1)\\\\\\\\n   • S3 Storage: 1 GB × $0.023 ≈ $0.02\\\\\\\\n   • S3 GET/PUT: 500 K GET, 5 K PUT ≈ $0.05\\\\\\\\n   • CloudFront: 10 GB data out × $0.085 ≈ $0.85\\\\\\\\n   • Lambda “news-builder”: <1 K invocations, 128 MB, 1 sec ≈ $0.00 (free tier likely covers)\\\\\\\\n   • ACM & Route 53 alias: $0 (certificate) + $0.50 per hosted zone.\\\\\\\\n   • TOTAL ≈ $1.42 / month (+$0.50 per extra GB egress).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nC. Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Create S3 bucket ‘hoa-site-bucket-<acct-id>’ with static-website hosting ON and public access BLOCKED (Origin Access Control will be used).\\\\\\\\n2. Upload placeholder index.html.\\\\\\\\n3. Create CloudFront OAC for bucket; default root object index.html; configure error to /index.html; attach.\\\\\\\\n4. Request ACM cert for ‘hoa-example.com’ + ‘www.hoa-example.com’ (Region us-east-1).\\\\\\\\n5. Create Route 53 hosted zone ‘hoa-example.com’.\\\\\\\\n6. After cert validation, update CloudFront distribution with ACM cert & custom domain.\\\\\\\\n7. Create A / AAAA Alias records to CloudFront distribution.\\\\\\\\n8. Create Cognito User Pool domain (if not already) and configure callback URL ‘https://www.hoa-example.com/auth/callback’ and logout URL ‘https://www.hoa-example.com’.\\\\\\\\n9. Add CloudFront Function to set/clear auth cookies post-callback.\\\\\\\\n10. Implement React SPA (src/*) with route guards; commit to CodeCommit.\\\\\\\\n11. CDK stack defines S3 bucket, CloudFront, Route 53, Lambda news-builder, S3 triggers, CodePipeline & CodeBuild.\\\\\\\\n12. Deploy CDK stack via cdk deploy.\\\\\\\\n13. Push initial code to trigger CodePipeline and publish site.\\\\\\\\n14. Test login, restricted pages, news upload workflow, contact form.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nD. Required Subordinate Roles / Files (Supervisors to Recruit)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Supervisor – UI/UX & React SPA Implementation\\\\\\\\n   • Deliver src/pages, routing guard, responsive design, ARIA labels, Tailwind config.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Supervisor – CMS Workflow & Lambda ‘news-builder’\\\\\\\\n   • Author Lambda (Node 18) that converts markdown to HTML using unified + remark-html, writes outputs, invalidates CloudFront.\\\\\\\\n   • Produce unit tests & CDK constructs for S3 → Lambda trigger.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Supervisor – CI/CD & IaC (CDK)\\\\\\\\n   • Build CDK stacks for bucket, OAC, CloudFront, Route 53 records, CodePipeline, CodeBuild, Cognito callback URIs.\\\\\\\\n   • Ensure DEP steps 1-13 map 1:1 into CDK.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. (Optional) Supervisor – Security & Compliance Review\\\\\\\\n   • Validate cookie settings, CSP headers via CloudFront Functions, penetration test public vs. private paths.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nE. Clarifications Needed\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Desired primary domain name (e.g., hoa-example.com) so we can request ACM cert and Route 53 zone.\\\\\\\\n2. Approximate expected monthly bandwidth (>50 GB?) to refine CloudFront cost estimate.\\\\\\\\n3. Whether a custom sub-domain (docs.hoa-example.com) is preferred for documents dashboard.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:13:33.198Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###clarq2###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"Our Directors have produced comprehensive architecture plans but require a few business-level decisions from you before we translate the plans into detailed, executable infrastructure steps. Clarifying these points will keep costs low, align email and domain configurations with your preferences, and ensure we satisfy any compliance obligations.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"1. Which Internet domain name would you like the HOA website to use (e.g., my-hoa.org, neighborhoodhoa.com)?  If you already own a domain, please specify it; if not, tell us the name you’d like so we can register or instruct you on doing so.\\\",\\n    \\\"2. What email address (or addresses) should receive messages submitted through the website’s Contact form?  For example, a single address like board@example.com or several individual addresses.\\\",\\n    \\\"3. Do you know of any specific compliance or data-privacy standards the site must meet (HIPAA, PCI, GDPR, etc.), or is basic industry-standard security sufficient for your needs?\\\",\\n    \\\"4. Roughly how much web traffic (data downloaded by visitors) do you expect each month—less than 50 GB, 50-200 GB, or more?  A ball-park figure helps us fine-tune cost estimates.\\\",\\n    \\\"5. Would you prefer the resident-only document area to live under a separate sub-domain such as docs.my-hoa.org, or simply keep everything under the main domain (e.g., my-hoa.org/documents)?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###clarq2###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:15:07.123Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"clarq2\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"1. Which Internet domain name would you like the HOA website to use (e.g., my-hoa.org, neighborhoodhoa.com)?  If you already own a domain, please specify it; if not, tell us the name you’d like so we can register or instruct you on doing so.\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"dunbaroakshoa.com\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"2. What email address (or addresses) should receive messages submitted through the website’s Contact form?  For example, a single address like board@example.com or several individual addresses.\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"admin@dunbaroakshoa.com\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"3. Do you know of any specific compliance or data-privacy standards the site must meet (HIPAA, PCI, GDPR, etc.), or is basic industry-standard security sufficient for your needs?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I do not know, probably just basic\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"4. Roughly how much web traffic (data downloaded by visitors) do you expect each month—less than 50 GB, 50-200 GB, or more?  A ball-park figure helps us fine-tune cost estimates.\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Sadly I barely know what a GB is\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"5. Would you prefer the resident-only document area to live under a separate sub-domain such as docs.my-hoa.org, or simply keep everything under the main domain (e.g., my-hoa.org/documents)?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Everything under one domain for sure please\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:15:07.123Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###order_backend_finalize###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Motti, the User has provided the missing details:\\\\n• Root domain: dunbaroakshoa.com (single-domain model; no separate sub-domain for documents).\\\\n• Contact-form recipient address: admin@dunbaroakshoa.com.\\\\n• Compliance: basic industry-standard security (no special regimes).\\\\n• Traffic: assume very low (< 50 GB data egress per month).\\\\n\\\\nPlease:\\\\n1. Update your Planning-phase report and DEP so all resource names, SES identities, bucket names, and email flows reflect dunbaroakshoa.com and admin@dunbaroakshoa.com.\\\\n2. Adjust cost numbers if any values change given the clarified low traffic.\\\\n3. Convert the high-level DEP into a fully deterministic, step-ordered DEP section ready for eventual PHASE submission (i.e., remove remaining <placeholders> by specifying how their values will be captured for later steps).\\\\n4. Begin Execution-phase readiness by RECRUITing the Supervisor-rank agents you listed (S-1 through S-6) and delegating their exact deliverables (one file / domain each). Feel free to combine recruitment + initial task instructions in a single response.\\\\n5. Return everything in a single REPORT call using sequence ID backend_finalize_v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"3\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"backend_finalize_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_backend_finalize###\\n\\n###order_frontend_finalize###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Valerius, the User’s clarifications are:\\\\n• Domain: dunbaroakshoa.com (keep all content—including resident documents—under this root domain, i.e., dunbaroakshoa.com/documents).\\\\n• Contact-form email: admin@dunbaroakshoa.com.\\\\n• Compliance: basic industry-stan